  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1238 PM EST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nTo be honest, there's not a lot to talk about weather wise over the \ncoming week. While precip comes to an end today, deep upper \ntroughing centered over the northeast CONUS will continue into \nSunday, resulting in temperatures remaining some 10-15 degrees below \nnormal. Said upper troughing will gradually eject northeastward \nMonday, leading way to an expected warming trend as upper ridging \nbuilds to our west and eventually shifts this direction by mid week. \nIn fact, as a result of the anomalously strong ridging forecast to \nbuild over the southeast CONUS, temperatures will actually rebound \nto 5-10 degrees above normal by the second half of the week.\n\nOn the precipitation side of things, it looks quite dry. A trough \nejecting from the Rockies into the upper midwest Thu into Fri may \nflatten the aforementioned upper ridge enough to allow for some \nlight precip to make it into the region on Fri. However, the overall \nblocking pattern aloft would suggest that rain has very low \nprobabilities of making it to our CWA. The lack of agreement in \nmodels, coupled with very light QPF in the ones that do show it, add \nconfidence to siding with continued dry conditions.",
